{"name":"Dried Saffron","hex":"#c33e29","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"dried","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Without water or moisture, said of something that has previously been wet or moist; resulting from the process of drying.","Usually of foods: cured, preserved by drying."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["simple past and past participle of dry"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/dried"},{"term":"saffron","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A plant of species Crocus sativus, a crocus.","A spice (seasoning) and colouring agent made from the stigma and part of the style of the plant, sometimes or formerly also used as a dye and insect repellent."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having an orange-yellow colour.","Associated with Hinduism, Hindus or Hindu nationalism."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/saffron"}],"entries":[{"title":"Saffron","blocks":[{"text":"Saffron is a spice derived from the sterile autumn-blooming flower of Crocus sativus, commonly known as the \"saffron crocus\". The vivid crimson stigma and styles are collected and dried for use mainly as a food seasoning and colourant. Saffron crocus was slowly propagated throughout much of Eurasia and later brought to parts of North Africa, North America, even as far as Fiji in Oceania by its Indian migrant descendant population."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/Saffron_-_premium_spice.jpg/330px-Saffron_-_premium_spice.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saffron"},{"title":"Saffron (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Saffron is a shade of yellow or orange, the colour of the tip of the saffron crocus thread, from which the spice saffron is derived. The hue of the spice saffron is primarily due to the carotenoid chemical crocin."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saffron_(color)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://